---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import FeatureHero from '../../components/FeatureHero.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CtaSection from '../../components/CtaSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../site.config';

const title = 'Schema governance';
const description = 'Apicurio Schema Registry with a JWT-aware RBAC proxy, Kafka-backed durability, and a Connect SMT that rewrites schema global IDs across mirrored clusters. Schemas that survive the trip.';

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Why Apicurio and not Confluent Schema Registry?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Apicurio is Apache 2.0, supports Avro / JSON Schema / Protobuf / OpenAPI / AsyncAPI, and has a clean storage-backend abstraction. Confluent Schema Registry is Confluent-Community-Licensed (source-available, restricted use).' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is kafkasql storage?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Apicurios native option that stores artifacts in a compacted Kafka topic. Scales with the cluster, no external database. The operator auto-provisions the journal topic and broker ACLs.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Do clients have to be schema-aware?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'For mirroring, yes — the rewrite SMT depends on the Apicurio V3 envelope format on the wire. Existing Avro + Apicurio clients already produce it.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can I use existing Apicurio policies?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Apicurio role-based access still works directly. The RBAC proxy adds per-artifact ACLs and JWT-claim binding on top.' } },
  ],
};

const apicurioYaml = `apiVersion: kafka.yavari.afshin.se/v1alpha1
kind: ApicurioRegistry
metadata:
  name: prod-registry
spec:
  clusterRef: prod
  storage:
    type: kafkasql              # mem | postgresql | kafkasql
  rbacProxy:
    enabled: true
    rbacRef: prod-rbac          # same KafkaRbac CR as the proxy
    oidc:
      jwksEndpointUrl: https://sso.example.com/realms/prod/protocol/openid-connect/certs
      groupsClaim: realm_access.roles
  externalAccess:
    mode: INGRESS
    host: schemas.example.com
    tlsSecretRef: schemas-tls
`;

const mm2Yaml = `apiVersion: kafka.yavari.afshin.se/v1alpha1
kind: MirrorMaker2
metadata:
  name: prod-eu-to-us
spec:
  source:
    clusterRef: prod-eu
    schemaRegistry:
      url: https://schemas-eu.example.com
      authSecretRef: mm2-eu-oidc        # OAuth2 client-credentials
  target:
    clusterRef: prod-us
    schemaRegistry:
      url: https://schemas-us.example.com
      authSecretRef: mm2-us-oidc
  schemaTransfer:
    enabled: true                       # rewrites V3 envelope globalIds per record
    cacheSize: 10000                    # LRU lookup cache
  topics:
    - orders
    - invoices
    - inventory-events
`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={faqSchema}>
  <FeatureHero
    eyebrow="Schema governance"
    title="Schemas that survive the trip."
    lede="Apicurio Schema Registry with a JWT-aware RBAC proxy and Kafka-backed durability — plus a Kafka Connect SMT that rewrites schema global IDs as records mirror across clusters."
    meta={['Apicurio 3.x', 'kafkasql / postgres / mem', 'V3 envelope-aware MM2', 'per-artifact RBAC']}
  />

  <section class="container-site py-16">
    <Prose>
      <h2 id="problem">Two problems Confluent left for you.</h2>
      <p>
        Most teams adopting a schema registry hit the same two walls. First:
        <strong>nobody enforces who can write which schemas</strong>. Apicurio
        and Confluent Schema Registry both expose REST APIs that any
        authenticated principal can call. Second: <strong>mirroring breaks
        schemas</strong>. The schema-id in the record header is local to the
        source registry; the target registry assigns different ids; consumers
        decode garbage.
      </p>
      <p>
        {site.brand} fixes both. The same JWT-claim-based RBAC that gates
        Kafka topics gates schema-registry artifacts. And a custom Kafka
        Connect Single-Message-Transform translates Apicurio V3 envelope
        global IDs as records flow through MirrorMaker2.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rbac-proxy">RBAC at the registry.</h2>
      <p>
        Apicurio runs behind an HTTP proxy written specifically for this
        purpose. The proxy:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Validates the bearer token against the OIDC issuer's JWKS.</li>
        <li>Pulls <code>realm_access.roles</code> (configurable claim) into a group set.</li>
        <li>Matches the request path against per-artifact ACLs in the <code>KafkaRbac</code> CR.</li>
        <li>Hot-reloads policy from a watched ConfigMap — no restart for ACL changes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Artifact-level <code>READ</code> / <code>WRITE</code> / <code>DELETE</code>
        actions, glob patterns, and the same wildcard semantics as the Kafka
        side.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-10 max-w-prose">
      <CodeBlock code={apicurioYaml} lang="yaml" filename="apicurio.yaml" />
    </div>

    <Prose>
      <h2 id="storage">Storage modes.</h2>
      <p>
        <code>kafkasql</code> backs artifacts with a compacted Kafka topic
        — durability comes from the cluster you're already running, scales
        with broker capacity, no external database to operate. The operator
        provisions the journal topic and broker ACLs automatically.
        <code>postgresql</code> and <code>mem</code> are also supported.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mm2">MirrorMaker2 that actually mirrors schemas.</h2>
      <p>
        The Apicurio V3 record envelope encodes <em>global IDs</em> — registry-
        local integers — directly in the message payload. Mirror a record
        from registry A to registry B and the global ID points to the wrong
        schema (or none). Consumers fail to deserialize.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>ApicurioSchemaTransferSmt</code> intercepts every record on the
        Connect worker, parses the envelope, fetches the source schema (with
        recursive reference resolution), looks up or creates an equivalent
        schema in the target registry, and rewrites the global ID in place.
        An LRU cache keeps the hot path off the network.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-10 max-w-prose">
      <CodeBlock code={mm2Yaml} lang="yaml" filename="mm2.yaml" />
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-prose">
      <Callout title="vs. Confluent Replicator / Strimzi MM2">
        <p>
          Confluent Replicator does schema migration via Confluent Schema
          Registry's exporter — and only for Avro. Strimzi MM2 does no schema
          rewriting at all; you coordinate IDs manually or rely on schema
          context features that aren't widely supported.
        </p>
        <p>
          {site.brand}'s SMT works for Avro, JSON Schema, and Protobuf via
          Apicurio, with reference resolution included. It's open source and
          lives in the same operator repo.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaSection />
</Base>
